## Q8 – Size It Up

The estimated development timeframe for the complete project is approximately three to four months.

The team's main priority is developing an effective repair ticket application with a straightforward submission and tracking process.

By keeping the application anonymous, we can avoid the additional development work associated with user profiles.

We also plan to exclude messaging functionality and paid features, helping reduce the project's overall complexity.

Integrating Fix My Campus into a larger student application would require additional time and resources and is not currently a priority.

### SA04 Release Planning

For the SA04 assignment, the release deadline is fixed at the end of semester Week 12.

The team must create a release plan using one-week sprints, estimated velocity and prioritised user stories. Any functionality that cannot realistically be completed by Week 12 will be moved to a later release.

*The final sprint allocation, story points and release burndown chart must be added after the release planning meeting.*

## Q9 – What's Going to Give?

The team has identified the following project priorities:

| Factor | Priority | Explanation |
|---|---|---|
| Quality | Highest | The application must work reliably and avoid significant errors. |
| Ease of Use | Highest | Students and staff should be able to report issues without difficulty. |
| Time | Medium | The team aims to deliver the core features within the agreed timeframe. |
| Budget | Medium | Free tools and hosting services will help reduce costs. |
| Scope | Flexible | Additional features may be excluded to prioritise the main reporting system. |

### Main Trade-Off

The team will prioritise quality and ease of use over additional functionality.

The application's main purpose is to allow users to submit and track campus maintenance requests.

Regular progress updates and team meetings will help ensure the project stays organised.

